---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Stamp from "../components/Stamp.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/sawungrana/lantipscript";
const RELEASES = `${REPO}/releases`;

const builds = [
  {
    name: "Full",
    when: "your computer is recent — an Apple silicon Mac, or a PC with a working GPU",
    speed: "best accuracy",
    size: "538 MB",
  },
  {
    name: "Lite",
    when: "your computer is older, or you're not sure",
    speed: "fast, close to Full",
    size: "204 MB",
  },
  {
    name: "Express",
    when: "the download needs to be small, or you want a rough first pass",
    speed: "fastest, least accurate",
    size: "56 MB",
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="lantipscript — a transcriber that stays on your computer"
  description="LantipScript turns interview recordings into text entirely on your machine. Fully offline, macOS and Windows, no account, no telemetry. v1.0.0."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 pt-12">
    <p class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">tools</p>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
      <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] leading-none text-ink-deep">LantipScript</h1>
      <Stamp label="v1.0.0" tone="accent" />
      <Stamp label="macos + windows" />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-[1.15rem]">
      A transcriber that stays on your computer. Drop in an interview recording; the text
      appears beside it; nothing is uploaded, and there's no account to make.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <a
        href={RELEASES}
        class="stamp-text border border-accent bg-accent-bright/10 px-4 py-2.5 text-accent no-underline hover:bg-accent-bright/20"
      >
        download — github releases ↗
      </a>
      <a
        href={REPO}
        class="stamp-text border border-rule-strong px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-soft no-underline hover:text-accent"
      >
        source ↗
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="stamp-text mt-3 text-ink-soft">
      installers are attached to the releases page · the builds differ only in the listening model they carry
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-12">
      <div class="plate-frame">
        <img
          src="/plates/banda-roadstead.webp"
          width="1325"
          height="828"
          alt="Engraving of the harbor at Banda Neira: sailing ships at anchor below a smoking volcano"
          class="block h-auto w-full"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption class="stamp-text mt-3 text-ink-soft">
        the banda roadstead — rijksmuseum, cc0
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">What it doesn't do</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>No model screen, no download button, no account, no telemetry.</li>
        <li>Your audio, the transcript, and every edit stay on your hard drive.</li>
        <li>
          Works on a plane, in a basement office, anywhere the internet doesn't reach.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4">
        Most apps that call themselves local still ask you to download a model before the
        first run. LantipScript doesn't — the listening model comes inside the app, chosen
        once when you pick your build.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">Pick a build</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-ink-soft">
        All builds are the same app, and all of them are fully offline. The only difference
        is the listening model they carry, and how much of your download it costs.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[36rem] border-collapse text-[0.98rem]">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
              <th class="stamp-text py-2 pr-4 font-normal text-ink-soft">build</th>
              <th class="stamp-text py-2 pr-4 font-normal text-ink-soft">choose it when</th>
              <th class="stamp-text py-2 pr-4 font-normal text-ink-soft">speed</th>
              <th class="stamp-text py-2 font-normal text-ink-soft">download</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              builds.map((b) => (
                <tr class="border-b rule-line">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink-deep">{b.name}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4">{b.when}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4">{b.speed}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 font-mono text-[0.85rem]">{b.size}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl">
        If you'd rather not decide: Full if the machine is new, Lite if it isn't. The gear
        icon shows which build is running and whether it got GPU acceleration.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">Using it</h2>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>Drag an audio or video file onto the window — mp3, wav, m4a, mp4, mov.</li>
        <li>Watch the progress bar. Nothing leaves the machine while it works.</li>
        <li>Click any line to jump the recording to that moment; edit the text directly.</li>
        <li>Export a plain .txt with timestamps, tidy for NVivo or Atlas.ti.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4">English and Bahasa Indonesia are detected automatically.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">The honest limits</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          Express carries the smallest model, so it mishears more — especially in Bahasa
          Indonesia. Treat it as a draft, and let Lite or Full do the real transcript.
        </li>
        <li>
          Without a GPU the app still runs, CPU-only and slower. The gear icon will tell you
          which one you got.
        </li>
        <li>
          Transcription is not summarisation or analysis. It gives you a careful text of
          what was said, and nothing more.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-[1.5rem] text-ink-deep">Building it yourself</h2>
      <p class="mt-4">
        You can, and the whole thing keeps running offline when you do. The
        <a href={REPO}>repository README</a> has the full recipe: bring one Whisper model
        (<code class="font-mono text-[0.85rem]">docs/MODELS.md</code> lists URLs and
        checksums), drop a static FFmpeg binary beside it, and run with
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.85rem]">--features metal</code> or
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.85rem]">--features vulkan</code> for GPU
        acceleration. Without a feature flag it still runs, CPU-only.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Rule />

    <section class="max-w-2xl pb-4">
      <p>
        LantipScript is named after my son. It was made for my own interview recordings, and
        it tries to stay as calm and as private as research deserves.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
